  <h1>Common Nursing Home Neglect Misconceptions</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The arbitration clause means I can&rsquo;t sue.&rdquo;</h2><p>Not necessarily. Many nursing home arbitration clauses are challenged successfully: signed under duress, signed by a family member without legal authority, or procedurally unfair. Challenge the clause through your attorney before assuming it&rsquo;s enforceable.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;Falls are just accidents — it&rsquo;s part of aging.&rdquo;</h2><p>Falls are preventable in the majority of nursing home cases. Facilities are required to assess fall risk and implement individualized prevention plans. A fall resulting from inadequate supervision or failure to follow the care plan is neglect, not an &ldquo;accident.&rdquo;</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;There&rsquo;s a cap on damages for elderly victims.&rdquo;</h2><p>Some states cap non-economic damages in medical malpractice cases. But elder abuse statute claims often bypass these caps. Punitive damages for intentional abuse are generally uncapped. The damages available depend on which legal theories are pursued.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;The facility will cover it up.&rdquo;</h2><p>Facilities do sometimes alter records. This is why you request records immediately and in writing. Altered records can be forensically detected and, when proven, constitute spoliation — which courts can instruct juries to infer negatively against the facility.</p>
  <h2>&ldquo;My loved one is gone — there&rsquo;s nothing I can do.&rdquo;</h2><p>Wrongful death and survival actions allow the estate and surviving family members to pursue claims after death. These claims can produce substantial recovery and hold facilities accountable to prevent future harm to other residents.</p>
